WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:05.470
Last lecture we stayed in the elastic region, where the specimen springs back.

2
00:00:05.470 --> 00:00:09.195
Now we keep pulling. What happens past the elastic limit?

3
00:00:12.315 --> 00:00:15.838
Past the yield point, deformation becomes plastic.

4
00:00:15.838 --> 00:00:21.324
Stress and strain are no longer proportional, and the deformation is not reversible.

5
00:00:21.744 --> 00:00:25.731
Here is why. Under load, the bonds first stretch, and then

6
00:00:25.731 --> 00:00:28.516
whole planes of atoms slide over each other.

7
00:00:28.516 --> 00:00:32.011
In crystals, that sliding is carried by dislocations.

8
00:00:32.430 --> 00:00:36.691
Now unload. The stretched bonds relax, but the planes stay sheared.

9
00:00:36.691 --> 00:00:38.245
Plastic means permanent.

10
00:00:42.345 --> 00:00:46.499
The straight part of the curve ends at the proportional limit, P.

11
00:00:46.499 --> 00:00:49.177
Beyond P, strain is no longer proportional to

12
00:00:49.177 --> 00:00:52.293
stress, and Hooke's law can no longer be used.

13
00:00:52.713 --> 00:00:57.523
P is hard to pin down, so the yield strength is defined with an offset.

14
00:00:57.523 --> 00:01:00.222
Draw a line parallel to the elastic slope,

15
00:01:00.222 --> 00:01:03.507
starting at a strain of zero point zero zero two.

16
00:01:03.507 --> 00:01:06.029
Where it meets the curve is sigma y.

17
00:01:06.449 --> 00:01:08.589
The two points are close to each other, but

18
00:01:08.589 --> 00:01:10.774
they are not the same point on the curve.

19
00:01:11.594 --> 00:01:13.672
Low carbon steel is different.

20
00:01:13.672 --> 00:01:16.872
It rises to an upper yield point, drops suddenly,

21
00:01:16.872 --> 00:01:19.905
and then runs along a jagged lower yield point.

22
00:01:19.905 --> 00:01:24.004
Its yield strength is the average stress at the lower yield point.

23
00:01:27.904 --> 00:01:30.895
Load a specimen past yield, then release it.

24
00:01:30.895 --> 00:01:35.844
It comes back down along a straight line, parallel to the original elastic line.

25
00:01:36.264 --> 00:01:39.733
It gives back only the elastic strain, sigma over E.

26
00:01:39.733 --> 00:01:41.716
That is the elastic recovery.

27
00:01:41.716 --> 00:01:43.698
Everything else is permanent.

28
00:01:44.117 --> 00:01:47.739
Now reload. The specimen stays elastic all the way up to

29
00:01:47.739 --> 00:01:50.959
where we stopped, and only then yields again.

30
00:01:50.959 --> 00:01:55.328
Its yield strength has gone up, from sigma y zero to sigma y one.

31
00:01:55.328 --> 00:01:57.168
That is strain hardening.

32
00:02:01.069 --> 00:02:03.528
Let us follow a full test to the end.

33
00:02:03.528 --> 00:02:06.491
As plastic deformation continues, the force keeps

34
00:02:06.491 --> 00:02:09.397
rising, because the metal strain hardens.

35
00:02:09.817 --> 00:02:13.673
At the top of the curve, the tensile strength, a neck forms.

36
00:02:13.673 --> 00:02:16.346
Stress concentrates in the neck, voids form,

37
00:02:16.346 --> 00:02:18.916
and they grow until the specimen fractures.

38
00:02:19.936 --> 00:02:22.225
For structural design, the yield stress

39
00:02:22.225 --> 00:02:25.100
usually matters more than the tensile strength.

40
00:02:25.100 --> 00:02:29.571
Once you pass it, the part has already deformed beyond acceptable limits.

41
00:02:33.471 --> 00:02:36.989
After the tensile strength, the engineering curve turns down.

42
00:02:36.989 --> 00:02:39.578
Does that mean the specimen is getting weaker?

43
00:02:39.997 --> 00:02:44.857
No. Engineering stress divides the force by the original area, A zero.

44
00:02:44.857 --> 00:02:48.170
But in the neck, the real area is shrinking fast.

45
00:02:48.590 --> 00:02:52.460
Divide by the actual area instead, and you get the true stress.

46
00:02:52.460 --> 00:02:54.918
It keeps rising all the way to fracture.

47
00:02:54.918 --> 00:02:58.735
So the true fracture strength is higher than the tensile strength.

48
00:02:59.156 --> 00:03:04.709
Before necking, the volume stays constant, so sigma T equals sigma times one

49
00:03:04.709 --> 00:03:09.866
plus epsilon, and epsilon T is the natural log of one plus epsilon.

50
00:03:10.285 --> 00:03:15.258
True values are more accurate, but the area is hard to measure during a test.

51
00:03:15.258 --> 00:03:18.422
So engineering values are what we usually report.

52
00:03:22.222 --> 00:03:25.467
Your lecture's example: a brass specimen.

53
00:03:25.467 --> 00:03:29.211
In the inset, the elastic line reaches one hundred fifty

54
00:03:29.211 --> 00:03:33.142
megapascals at a strain of zero point zero zero one six.

55
00:03:33.142 --> 00:03:36.761
So E is about ninety three point eight gigapascals.

56
00:03:37.181 --> 00:03:40.163
The zero point zero zero two offset line meets the

57
00:03:40.163 --> 00:03:45.352
curve at about two hundred fifty megapascals. That is the yield strength.

58
00:03:45.772 --> 00:03:49.635
The maximum load uses the tensile strength, four hundred fifty

59
00:03:49.635 --> 00:03:54.552
megapascals, on the original area of a twelve point eight millimeter rod.

60
00:03:54.552 --> 00:03:58.239
That is about fifty seven thousand nine hundred newtons.

61
00:03:58.659 --> 00:04:01.261
At three hundred forty five megapascals we are

62
00:04:01.261 --> 00:04:04.259
far past yield, so do not use Hooke's law.

63
00:04:04.259 --> 00:04:08.161
Read the strain from the curve, about zero point zero six.

64
00:04:08.161 --> 00:04:12.347
The two hundred fifty millimeter bar stretches fifteen millimeters.

65
00:04:16.247 --> 00:04:21.264
In the plastic region, the true stress strain curve follows a power law.

66
00:04:21.264 --> 00:04:24.226
Sigma T equals K times epsilon T to the n.

67
00:04:24.646 --> 00:04:29.055
n is the strain hardening exponent, from about zero point one five

68
00:04:29.055 --> 00:04:33.149
for some steels to zero point five for some copper alloys.

69
00:04:33.149 --> 00:04:35.479
K is the strength coefficient.

70
00:04:36.299 --> 00:04:38.501
Take logs, and the power law becomes a

71
00:04:38.501 --> 00:04:41.490
straight line on a log log plot, with slope n.

72
00:04:41.490 --> 00:04:45.789
The higher the slope, the stronger the material gets as it is strained.

73
00:04:46.209 --> 00:04:51.331
Annealed copper has n equal to zero point five four, and hardens the most.

74
00:04:51.331 --> 00:04:55.308
Alloy steel forty three forty has only zero point one five.

75
00:04:55.729 --> 00:04:59.991
For example, low carbon steel, with K of five hundred thirty

76
00:04:59.991 --> 00:05:04.692
megapascals and n of zero point two six, needs about two hundred ninety

77
00:05:04.692 --> 00:05:09.268
one megapascals of true stress at a true strain of zero point one.

78
00:05:13.068 --> 00:05:18.899
Finally, design. Loads and properties are uncertain, so we never push the limit.

79
00:05:18.899 --> 00:05:22.329
The working stress is the yield strength divided by a factor

80
00:05:22.329 --> 00:05:26.216
of safety, N, usually between one point two and four.

81
00:05:26.635 --> 00:05:30.273
Your lecture's example: a ten forty five steel rod with a

82
00:05:30.273 --> 00:05:33.791
yield strength of three hundred ten megapascals carries two

83
00:05:33.791 --> 00:05:37.667
hundred twenty thousand newtons, with N equal to five.

84
00:05:37.667 --> 00:05:40.708
The working stress is sixty two megapascals.

85
00:05:41.128 --> 00:05:45.237
The area must be two hundred twenty thousand newtons over sixty two

86
00:05:45.237 --> 00:05:50.695
megapascals: three point five five times ten to the minus three square meters.

87
00:05:50.695 --> 00:05:54.375
So the diameter is about six point seven centimeters.

88
00:05:54.795 --> 00:05:58.086
Design with the tensile strength instead, and you would get

89
00:05:58.086 --> 00:06:01.430
about five centimeters: a rod that yields in service.

90
00:06:05.330 --> 00:06:07.195
Here is the summary of topic ten.

91
00:06:07.615 --> 00:06:12.730
Plastic deformation is permanent, and unloading gives back only the elastic strain.

92
00:06:13.150 --> 00:06:15.664
Yield comes from the zero point zero zero two

93
00:06:15.664 --> 00:06:18.458
offset, and strain hardening raises it.

94
00:06:18.878 --> 00:06:21.637
Necking, voids and fracture follow the tensile

95
00:06:21.637 --> 00:06:24.838
strength, and true stress uses the actual area.

96
00:06:25.258 --> 00:06:29.892
And the power law, the brass example, and the safety factor tie it together.

97
00:06:29.892 --> 00:06:32.337
Next lecture: toughness and hardness.
